<h2 id="overview">Understanding Vehicle Titles</h2>

<p>A vehicle title is the legal document that establishes ownership of a motor vehicle. Issued by the state in which the vehicle is registered, the title records the owner's name, the vehicle's VIN, make, model, year, and any liens held against it. While all 50 states issue vehicle titles, the requirements, fees, processes, and title branding categories vary significantly from state to state.</p>

<p>Understanding these differences is critical for anyone buying or selling a vehicle across state lines, moving to a new state, or evaluating a vehicle with a branded title. This guide compares the key title rules across all 50 US states. For state-specific details, visit our <a href="/states/">state pages</a>.</p>

<h2 id="title-transfer">Title Transfer Requirements</h2>

<p>When a vehicle is sold, the title must be transferred from seller to buyer. Every state requires:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Signed title</strong> with seller's signature, buyer's name, sale price, and date</li>
  <li><strong>Odometer disclosure</strong> (required for vehicles less than 20 years old under federal law, 49 USC &sect;32705)</li>
  <li><strong>Application for new title</strong> filed by the buyer at the state DMV or equivalent agency</li>
  <li><strong>Title fee payment</strong> (ranges from $2 in Oregon to $164 in Montana)</li>
</ul>

<p>Additional state-specific requirements may include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bill of sale</strong> &mdash; Required in approximately 35 states as a separate document from the title assignment</li>
  <li><strong>VIN inspection</strong> &mdash; Required in approximately 15 states for out-of-state vehicles. The VIN on the vehicle is physically verified against the title. Use Car Alpha's <a href="/tools/vin-decoder/">VIN decoder</a> to verify your VIN matches the title specs before inspection.</li>
  <li><strong>Emissions/safety inspection</strong> &mdash; Required in approximately 20 states before title transfer can be completed</li>
  <li><strong>Lien release documentation</strong> &mdash; If the vehicle had financing, the lienholder must provide a release before a clean title can be issued</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="title-branding">Title Branding Rules by State</h2>

<p>Title branding is a critical consumer protection mechanism. When a vehicle has been significantly damaged, deemed a total loss by an insurer, or returned under lemon law, the title is "branded" with a designation that permanently follows the vehicle. However, the branding categories and thresholds vary dramatically between states:</p>

<h3>Common Title Brands</h3>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Brand</th><th>Meaning</th><th>States Using This Brand</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Salvage</td><td>Declared total loss by insurer; damage exceeds a percentage of vehicle value</td><td>All 50 states</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rebuilt</td><td>Previously salvaged vehicle that has been repaired and re-inspected</td><td>~45 states</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Flood</td><td>Damaged by water/flooding</td><td>~35 states</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Lemon</td><td>Returned under state lemon law</td><td>~30 states</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Junk</td><td>Not repairable or not economical to repair</td><td>~40 states</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Theft Recovery</td><td>Stolen and recovered, possibly stripped or damaged</td><td>~25 states</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Odometer Discrepancy</td><td>Reported mileage does not match known history</td><td>All 50 states</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Salvage Threshold Comparison</h3>
<p>The damage threshold at which a vehicle receives a salvage title varies by state:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>60% of value:</strong> Texas</li>
  <li><strong>70% of value:</strong> New York</li>
  <li><strong>75% of value:</strong> California, Florida, Ohio, Pennsylvania</li>
  <li><strong>80% of value:</strong> Michigan, Illinois</li>
  <li><strong>Total loss only (insurer declared):</strong> Some states defer entirely to insurer determination</li>
</ul>

<p>This means a vehicle totaled in Texas (60% threshold) might receive a clean title in Michigan (80% threshold). This inconsistency is what enables title washing. See the <a href="#title-washing">title washing section</a> below.</p>

<h2 id="title-fees">Title Fees Comparison</h2>

<p>Title fees range significantly across states. Here are some notable examples:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>State</th><th>Title Fee</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Oregon</td><td>$98.50</td><td>Includes registration</td></tr>
    <tr><td>California</td><td>$23</td><td>Plus use tax on purchase price</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Texas</td><td>$33</td><td>Plus motor vehicle sales tax (6.25%)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Florida</td><td>$75.25</td><td>Includes initial registration</td></tr>
    <tr><td>New York</td><td>$50</td><td>Plus sales tax and plate fees</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Illinois</td><td>$150</td><td>Among the highest in the nation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ohio</td><td>$15</td><td>One of the lowest</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Michigan</td><td>$15</td><td>Plus plate transfer or new plate fee</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Note: Sales tax or use tax on the vehicle purchase price is separate from the title fee and applies in most states. Five states have no sales tax on vehicle purchases: Alaska, Delaware, Montana, New Hampshire, and Oregon.</p>

<h2 id="duplicate-titles">Getting a Duplicate Title</h2>

<p>If a title is lost, stolen, or damaged, every state provides a process for obtaining a duplicate. Requirements typically include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Application form (varies by state)</li>
  <li>Proof of identity (driver's license)</li>
  <li>Proof of ownership (registration, insurance, or previous title copy)</li>
  <li>VIN verification (in some states, a physical VIN inspection is required)</li>
  <li>Duplicate title fee ($5&ndash;$75 depending on state)</li>
</ul>

<p>Processing times range from same-day (at DMV offices in many states) to 4&ndash;6 weeks (for states that require central office processing). If you need to verify your VIN for a duplicate title application, use our <a href="/tools/vin-decoder/">free VIN decoder</a>.</p>

<h2 id="out-of-state">Out-of-State Title Transfers</h2>

<p>Moving to a new state or buying a vehicle from another state requires transferring the title to your new state of residence. Most states require this within 30&ndash;90 days of establishing residency or purchasing the vehicle.</p>

<p>The process typically involves:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Surrendering the out-of-state title to your new state's DMV</li>
  <li>Completing a title application in the new state</li>
  <li>Providing proof of insurance in the new state</li>
  <li>Passing any required inspections (VIN, emissions, safety)</li>
  <li>Paying the new state's title fee and any applicable taxes</li>
</ol>

<p>Important: Some states (approximately 15) require a physical VIN inspection for all out-of-state title transfers. This is a consumer protection measure to prevent VIN cloning and stolen vehicle transfers. The inspection verifies the VIN on the vehicle matches the VIN on the title.</p>

<h2 id="electronic-titles">Electronic Title States</h2>

<p>An increasing number of states now offer electronic titles (e-titles), where the title record exists only as a digital document in the state's database. States with full e-title programs include Florida, Virginia, Texas, Georgia, and California (partial). Benefits include faster transfers, no risk of physical loss, and reduced fraud potential.</p>

<p>For vehicles with e-titles, the transfer process is typically faster: the seller authorizes the transfer digitally, and the buyer receives the new electronic title within days rather than weeks.</p>

<h2 id="title-washing">Title Washing and Consumer Protection</h2>

<p>Title washing occurs when a vehicle with a branded title (salvage, flood, lemon) is re-registered in a state that does not recognize or transfer the original brand. The vehicle then receives a "clean" title in the new state, hiding its damage history from future buyers.</p>

<p>This is possible because title branding categories are not standardized across states. A vehicle branded as "flood" in one state may receive a clean title in a state that does not have a specific flood brand category.</p>

<h3>How to Protect Yourself</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Always decode the VIN.</strong> Verify the vehicle's year, make, model, and specs match what the seller claims. Use our <a href="/tools/vin-decoder/">free VIN decoder</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Check recall history.</strong> Extensive recall history, combined with a suspiciously low price, can indicate a problematic vehicle. Use our <a href="/recalls/">recall checker</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Request a vehicle history report.</strong> Services like <a href="/go/carfax/" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">Carfax</a> aggregate title records from all 50 states and can reveal brands that the current title may not show.</li>
  <li><strong>Get a pre-purchase inspection.</strong> A qualified <a href="/go/mechanics/" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">mechanic</a> can identify flood damage, structural repair, or other evidence of previous total loss.</li>
  <li><strong>Check the NMVTIS.</strong> The National Motor Vehicle Title Information System aggregates title data from all states and can reveal brands not shown on the current title.</li>
</ul>

<p>For detailed information on specific states, browse our <a href="/states/">state-by-state guides</a> covering registration, insurance, lemon law, and EV incentives.</p>

